# MoE OTD per-expert routing histogram (M10 Deliverable B(i))
#
# Adds a per-expert ROUTING histogram to OffloadExpertWeightProvider: how many
# times each expert id was routed to try_acquire_simultaneous, counted BEFORE
# any hit/miss or capacity-dedup logic runs, one increment per (call, expert
# id occurrence) - a repeat within one `experts` vector counts once per
# repeat, matching a real decode step routing the same expert to more than
# one token. Companion to patches/0004's evictions/acquisitions counters and
# to the M14 cpu_tier_* counters (patches/0011/0012): those all count slot
# resolutions or capacity outcomes, never plain routing, and the design has a
# named trap for anyone who tries to reconstruct routing from them after the
# fact (see below).
#
# Where the seam is (all three touched files are existing ones - see
# 0013.files - no new source file was added, so no CMake GLOB re-registration
# question arises for either the plugin target or ov_gpu_unit_tests):
#   - expert_weight_providers.hpp: OffloadExpertWeightProvider grows a
#     `~OffloadExpertWeightProvider() override` declaration, two public const
#     accessors (`routing_histogram()`, `layer_seq_id()`), and three private
#     members (`_routing_hist`, `_routing_hist_enabled`, `_layer_seq_id`).
#     ResidentExpertWeightProvider is untouched: MOE_OTD_ROUTING_HIST, like
#     MOE_OTD_PERF_LOG before it, is scoped to the offload (OTD) path, which
#     is the only one that has a cldnn::MOECompressed::Config (hence a
#     num_expert) at construction and the only one this milestone's counters
#     (moe_otd_perf.hpp) ever touch.
#   - expert_weight_providers.cpp:
#     - An anonymous namespace holds the process-wide sequence-id counter
#       (std::atomic<size_t>), the registry (mutex + vector<OffloadExpert-
#       WeightProvider*>, raw pointers per the task brief - not weak_ptr,
#       because providers are constructed as plain objects/stack objects in
#       existing tests and held via shared_ptr in production
#       (moe_3gemm_swiglu_opt.cpp:1339, one make_shared<...> per impl, shared
#       across clones at :1692 - never re-wrapped in a second shared_ptr a
#       weak_ptr could observe), and the dump function.
#     - OffloadExpertWeightProvider's constructor sizes `_routing_hist` to
#       `config.num_expert`, reads MOE_OTD_ROUTING_HIST once for this
#       provider (`_routing_hist_enabled`), takes the next id from the atomic
#       counter, and unconditionally registers `this` (registration is an id
#       + a vector push under a mutex - cheap regardless of whether counting
#       is on, see the "two different once" note below). The destructor
#       unregisters.
#     - The counting loop is the first thing try_acquire_simultaneous does,
#       above the dedup map (`expert_to_slot`) that everything else in that
#       function is built around.
#
# The "before the hit/miss split" argument, anchored in the tree this patch
# applies to (src/plugins/intel_gpu/src/graph/impls/ocl_v2/moe/
# expert_weight_providers.cpp, OffloadExpertWeightProvider::
# try_acquire_simultaneous, pre-existing code untouched by this patch except
# for the new loop above it):
#   The existing gpu_hits/gpu_misses counters (moe_otd_perf.hpp, wired in
#   both the pre-M14 and M14 branches of that function) are incremented once
#   per *slot resolution*: a repeat of the same expert within one call is
#   short-circuited by the `expert_to_slot.find(expert)` dedup check at the
#   top of the per-position loop and never reaches either counter a second
#   time, and an expert that stays resident across many calls is a hit every
#   time, so the miss counter (and evictions, patches/0004) undercounts it to
#   at most one compulsory miss no matter how often it is actually routed
#   afterward. A histogram built by post-processing those counters (or by
#   counting only inside the `else` "miss" arm, the naive place to add a
#   counter to this function) would therefore answer "which experts miss the
#   resident set", not "which experts does the router pick" - and the two
#   diverge most for exactly the popular experts the pool is big enough to
#   keep resident, which is the case this instrument exists to make visible.
#   Counting the raw `experts` vector before any of that logic runs is the
#   only version immune to it: every position counts exactly once, hit or
#   miss, resident or not.
#
# "Every caller" (decode batched, grouped, prefill) is not three separate
# counting sites: all three route through the same virtual
# try_acquire_simultaneous override (moe_3gemm_swiglu_opt.cpp:1046 decode
# batched with cpu_tier_misses, :1115 grouped, :1199 prefill's raw_topk_ids),
# so one counting loop at the top of the OffloadExpertWeightProvider override
# covers all three without touching any call site.
#
# Concurrency: plain (non-atomic) uint32_t increments. Anchor: lru_cache.cpp,
# the comment directly above LRUCache::probe() (arcint M14) - "a provider is
# driven by exactly one stream ... so there is no concurrent caller to race
# against" - the same invariant that already lets probe()'s own
# has_free_slot -> get_lru_item sequence be non-atomic across the two calls.
# try_acquire_simultaneous's own pre-existing state (the `expert_to_slot`
# unordered_map, `missed_experts`/`missed_slots` vectors, `_bound`/`_cpu_tier`
# member reads) already relies on the same single-stream-per-provider
# invariant without any lock; this patch counts the same way.
#
# Layer identity and the registry's two different "once"s (worth stating
# explicitly since they are easy to conflate): sequence-id assignment and
# registry registration are unconditional and read nothing from the
# environment beyond a single getenv() at construction
# (`_routing_hist_enabled`, decided per-provider, gates only the per-expert
# counting loop's cost). The *dump* condition (whether MOE_OTD_ROUTING_HIST
# is set) is instead read fresh, once, at the one point it is ever checked -
# inside the atexit handler, at real process exit - rather than cached
# process-wide the way get_perf_counters()'s `static bool enabled`
# (moe_otd_runtime.cpp) is. This is a deliberate departure from that
# precedent, not an inconsistency with it: get_perf_counters()'s cache has to
# be process-wide because its bool gates a counter increment checked on every
# acquire call across every provider, so caching once per process is the
# whole point of that cache. The dump condition here is checked exactly once
# per process (there is only one exit), so there is nothing to cache, and
# reading fresh means whether a real run's dump fires depends only on the
# environment at exit - never on which layer happened to be constructed
# first, or (in a test binary running many independent TEST bodies in one
# process) on some earlier test's provider having been built before this
# one's setenv() call. A process-wide cached bool here would have made the
# red-first unit tests below flaky by construction-order accident; reading
# fresh does not.
#
# Cost: config.num_expert (256 on the served shape) x 4 B = 1 KiB per layer;
# ~40 layers in a served model => ~40 KiB total. One increment per routed id
# when enabled; when MOE_OTD_ROUTING_HIST was unset at this provider's
# construction, try_acquire_simultaneous pays exactly one branch
# (`if (_routing_hist_enabled)`) per call and the loop body never runs -
# zero per-expert cost, no allocation on the hot path (the vector is sized
# once, at construction, from config.num_expert and never reallocated).
#
# Dump: opt-in via env MOE_OTD_ROUTING_HIST=<path>, same channel as
# MOE_OTD_PERF_LOG. At real process exit (atexit, registered once, same
# pattern as get_perf_counters()) writes a CSV to <path>: header
# "layer,expert,count", one row per (layer, expert) with a nonzero count, in
# layer-sequence order (the registry snapshot is sorted by layer_seq_id()
# before writing, defensively, rather than trusted to already be in that
# order), then a trailing "# total,<T>" comment line. Also prints
# "[OTD_PERF] routing_hist: layers=N experts=E total=T path=<path>" to
# stderr so a window log records that the dump fired (or, if the file could
# not be opened, a "failed to open <path> for writing" line instead, with no
# CSV written).
#
